    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/24670?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Interprofessional Presence (Part Time), Center for Simulation and Innovation (Phoenix)",
      "description": "Interprofessional Presence (Part Time), Center for Simulation and Innovation (Phoenix) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Interprofessional Presence (Part Time), Center for Simulation and Innovation (Phoenix) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req24670 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 12/3/2025 Back to Search Interprofessional Presence (Part Time), Center for Simulation and Innovation (Phoenix) Posting Number req24670 Department COM Phx Ugrd Medical Education Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 N. 5th Street, Phoenix, AZ 85004 USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix Center for Simulation and Innovation (CSI) is seeking qualified candidates to join our Simulation Pool Staff  (non-faculty). The CSI Pool Staff hourly positions include Simulation Operator and Interprofessional Presence (IPP). Our Center for Simulation and Innovation teaches medical students to be tomorrow’s doctors using safe practice with mannequins and task trainers. In this role, you would be a primary participant in clinical scenarios acting as a clinical nurse presence support (IPP) during Simulation events, operating high- and low-fidelity clinical simulation equipment, setting up and tearing down simulation event rooms, conducting tours of the facility and other duties as assigned. Knowledge of clinical healthcare practices, simulation, and related competencies are especially important to the IPP position as this knowledge is applied during Simulation events. Work shifts for this part time position are scheduled on an as needed basis and will vary in scope and complexity based upon your level of experience in accordance with CSI events, programs and projects. The shifts can be scheduled for up to 4-8 hours and the ideal expectation is that Pool Staff will work several four-hour shifts per month. Common event day schedules include Tuesdays, Wednesdays, Thursdays, and Fridays within normal business hours (8am-5pm).  Pool Staff self-select and sign up for offered hourly shifts using CSI's online scheduling and staffing systems, usually posted well in advance (more than two months out). Parking costs will be paid by the CSI in the designated Phoenix Biomedical Campus garage. This position does not include benefits. Please be sure to watch the videos on our website to get a better idea of who we are and how we contribute to the mission of the College: https://phoenixmed.arizona.edu/simcenter. Please Note: Applications are accepted year-round with hiring tiimes being based on the Center's hiring needs. We do not hire every month. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Interprofessional Presence primary role is to apply clinical experience and act as a clinical nurse presence support (IPP) during high and low fidelity clinical education scenarios using simulation and related technology. Responsible for setup, tear down, and quality assurance of simulation activities, special projects, and CSI events. Conduct tours of the facility and may teach procedures. Provide program and project support, training, and guidance to faculty and Simulation Staff including other Pool Staff members. Participate in event pre-brief and event de-brief meetings, including giving and receiving feedback in discussions with faculty, students, staff and management. May serve as event, project, or shift leader. May participate in clinical simulations as Simulation Operator. Other duties as assigned. Physical Requirements: Frequently lifts and carries up to 50 pounds (i.e. Mannequin, task trainers and supply boxes). Frequently bends/stoops, pushes/pulls, walks, and twists. Ability to bend, stoop and lift up to 50 lbs. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong communication skills. Experience planning and leading events, managing resources, and coaching employees. This job posting reflects the general nature and level of work expected of the selected candidate(s). It is not intended to be an exhaustive list of all duties and responsibilities. The institution reserves the right to amend or update this description as organizational priorities and institutional needs evolve. Minimum Qualifications Current unrestricted license or certification in good standing to practice as a(n) Registered Nurse (RN), Certified Emergency Paramedic (CEP), Licensed Practical Nurse (LPN), Emergency Medical Technician (EMT), medical assistant, Respiratory Therapist (RT), Physician Assistant (PA) or similar healthcare provider professional; OR, Three years’ prior experience as a(n) Registered Nurse (RN), Certified Emergency Paramedic (CEP), Licensed Practical Nurse (LPN), Emergency Medical Technician (EMT), medical assistant, Respiratory Therapist (RT), Physician Assistant (PA) or similar healthcare provider professional; OR, Any equivalent combination of experience, training and or/education. Preferred Qualifications Recent experience providing healthcare in a clinical setting. Previous experience in a hospital setting such as in pediatrics, obstetrics and gynecology, medical/surgical, peri-operative, telemetry, etc. Previous experience in a clinical simulation setting, actively engaged in simulation events. Mastery of Simulation Operator role. Proficient with web-based computer programs (Microsoft Suite and Teams, GoogleDocs, scheduling). FLSA Non-Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week Varies Job FTE 0.025 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $22.77 - $34.15 Compensation Type hourly rate Grade 6 Compensation Guidance The Rate of Pay Field represents the University of Arizona’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. The University considers several factors when extending an offer, including but not limited to, the role and associated responsibilities, a candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, and internal equity. The Grade Range represent a full range of career compensation growth over time. The university offers compensation growth opportunities within its career architecture. To learn more about compensation, please review our Applicant Compensation Guide and our Total Rewards Calculator . Career Stream and Level PC1 Job Family Med Education Prgm Job Function Academic Administration Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 0 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Talent Acquisition, Office of Human Resources talent@arizona.edu Open Date 12/3/2025 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Resume and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. Cover Letter: Should clearly indicate how your skills and professional employment experience meet the Minimum and the Preferred qualifications (if applicable). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/24668?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Part Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Clinical Skills Evaluator, Doctoring (College of Medicine-Phoenix)",
      "description": "Part Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Clinical Skills Evaluator, Doctoring (College of Medicine-Phoenix) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Part Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Clinical Skills Evaluator, Doctoring (College of Medicine-Phoenix) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req24668 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 12/3/2025 Back to Search Part Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Clinical Skills Evaluator, Doctoring (College of Medicine-Phoenix) Posting Number req24668 Department COM Phx Ugrd Medical Education Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Medical Sub-Speciality Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 N. 5th St, Phoenix, AZ 85004 USA Position Highlights The Center for Clinical Skills and Assessments is currently seeking a faculty Clinical Skills Evaluator for the Doctoring Program to teach and assess interviewing and physical exam skills to 1st and/or 2nd year UA medical students in a structured setting using standardized patients (SP). The Center is outfitted with video monitoring equipment in each of the 24 exam rooms. The space is equipped with LearningSpace, a web-based platform that records SP encounters and enables faculty to view them live and students to review them after the event, facilitating feedback and assessment.\nDuring each of the blocks, there is a concentration on a specific group of organ systems, except in the first block, when a cursory introduction to medical interviewing and the physical examination is presented. Therefore, the interview and physical exam elements emphasized are focused on those organ systems. The first year Doctoring Course has been divided into blocks: Block 1: Molecular Bases and Life and Disease (MBLD) Block 2: Musculoskeletal Neurologic (MSNS 1) Block 3: Neurological Sciences (NS 2) Block 4 Cardiovascular Hematology (CV-Heme) Block 5: Pulmonary Renal Acid Base (PRAB) With the academic year beginning the 1st week in July and ending mid-May.\n                                 Year 2 is compressed, beginning the 2nd week of July and ending mid-December. \n                                 The second year Doctoring Course has been divided into blocks: Block 1: GI, metabolism, Diabetes, Nutritional Science (GIMDN) Block 2: Reproduction, Endocrine, Behavior through the life span (REBLS) The class of 130 students has been divided into sections, with 1/3 attending on Tuesday, Wednesday, or Thursday afternoon from 12:45-5:45 pm, approximately every other week. Each of these groups is further divided into cohorts of three students each and assigned to a physician coach (Cohort Faculty). Assigned student-faculty pairings remain the same for the semester to ensure each student is monitored for adequate progress. Module Faculty members also assist in teaching Modules. Module Faculty are present at each session and are responsible for 6 students. The successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member in an appropriate department, actively participate in teaching, clinical service, and/or scholarly work, and must qualify for an appointment at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Biomedical Campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists, and leaders who are lifelong learners and inquisitive scholars. The Phoenix Biomedical Campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. This appointment is not eligible for tenure. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Attend all assigned Clinical Skills & Assessment\n     Sessions. Teach interviewing and physical exam skills to 1st\n     and/or 2nd year UA medical students in a structured setting using\n     standardized patients (SP). Prepare for each session by completing required reading\n     and viewing of video of the portions of the physical exam that will be\n     practiced at that particular session. Direct student learning with a focus on proficient\n     history taking using Patient-Centered Interviewing techniques. (including\n     emotion seeking and emotion validation tools and motivational interview\n     tools) Demonstrate physical examinations with efficiency and\n     score students accurately. Develop documentation skills to promote concise,\n     precise, valid entries into the medical record. Proficiently navigate and utilize SharePoint website\n     and LearningSpace application to access videos, view cases, and enter\n     assessments and evaluations. Demonstrate approach to critical thinking to enable the\n     student to develop both broad and then focused differentials based on the\n     standardized patient (SP) presentations. Additional duties may be assigned. Minimum Qualifications MD or DO. Board Certified at the time of appointment. Licensed to practice medicine in Arizona at the time\n       of appointment. Dedicated to delivery of patient-centered medicine. Individuals who are selected for employment and do\n       NOT currently hold a faculty title with The University of Arizona\n       College of Medicine-Phoenix, will be required to obtain faculty status.\n       This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the\n       appropriate College review committee. Able to commit to a designated afternoon either\n       Tuesday, Wednesday, or Thursday when class is scheduled. Preferred Qualifications At least one year of technical experience working in a remote or virtual environment using video conferencing software, preferably Zoom. Teaching experience in an academic environment at the undergraduate medical student level. Primary care specialty. Rank Tenure Information FLSA Non-Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week Varies Job FTE .025-.08 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $100. Per Hour Compensation Type hourly rate Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 0 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Talent Acquisition, Office of Human Resources talent@arizona.edu Open Date 12/3/2025 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. Cover Letter: Should clearly indicate how your skills and professional employment experience meet the Minimum and the Preferred qualifications (if applicable). If you currently hold a faculty appointment with the College of Medicine-Phoenix, please include the title in your letter. If you do NOT currently hold a faculty appointment, please include a statement indicating your interest in applying for a faculty title with The University of Arizona College of Medicine- Phoenix. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information, including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/24666?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Simulation Center Instructor (College of Medicine-Phoenix)",
      "description": "Part-Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Simulation Center Instructor (College of Medicine-Phoenix) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Part-Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Simulation Center Instructor (College of Medicine-Phoenix) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req24666 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 12/2/2025 Back to Search Part-Time Faculty (MD, DO) - Simulation Center Instructor (College of Medicine-Phoenix) Posting Number req24666 Department COM Phx Ugrd Medical Education Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Medical Sub-Speciality Location Greater Phoenix Area Address Phoenix, AZ USA Position Highlights The Center for Simulation and Innovation (CSI) at the University of Arizona, College of Medicine-Phoenix is a state-of-the art healthcare simulation facility dedicated, in every sense, to fulfill its mission to continually improving health care through education, research, and advancement of clinical care. The world-class 33,000 square-foot facility is designed to teach all healthcare professionals in a safe and realistic clinical environment. The center features multiple mock hospital rooms and resuscitation bays, surgical, obstetric and pediatric suites, debriefing rooms, and a myriad of high-fidelity mannequins, simulators, and synthetic task trainers.\nMembers of the Simulation Faculty Pool will have the opportunity to work alongside nationally recognized educators, researchers, and clinicians to help develop an innovative simulation curriculum to enhance medical student education and improve patient care. As part of the simulation team, Simulation Faculty Pool members will also enjoy the benefits of participating in faculty development sessions geared towards improving bedside education, enhancing clinical assessments, and integration of advanced medical technologies into clinical practice. Applications are accepted year-round with hiring times being based on the Center's staffing needs. We do not hire every month. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Simulation based training (SBT) is integrated into all four years of the medical student curriculum at the University of Arizona, College of Medicine-Phoenix. Medical students participate in simulation-based activities centered around small-group teaching with active discussions and debriefings, in addition to procedural training and practice. Eligible physicians are invited to apply for a Simulation Instructor position. Simulation Instructors will be provided with the opportunity to participate in and facilitate simulation based training events throughout the academic year. As a Simulation Instructor, physicians will be working alongside our Simulation Curriculum Directors, and our Block, Clerkship, Theme, and Course Directors to develop, review, and organize simulation scenarios used to train and educate our medical students. Simulation Instructors will participate in simulation scenarios and coordinate facilitated debriefing sessions following each scenario to enhance our medical student education. Our Simulation Instructors will also work closely with our simulation team to help coordinate simulation based educational activities, in addition to assisting with the recruitment and training of faculty volunteers participating in our simulation program. Simulation Instructors will be required to work a minimum of 25 hours in simulation per academic year, with a maximum of 200 hours per academic year.Simulation Instructors are strongly encouraged to participate in Student Interest Group Simulation Activities that occur throughout the academic year and to participate and attend curricular events and committee meetings as they arise. Physical Demands: Hear and listen attentively in a simulated environment where there are multiple auditory, visual, tactile, and verbal cues that require prompt responses. Vision capabilities required for this position include: close vision and ability to work on computers. Simulation Instructors must be able to see and interpret computer screens, patient monitors, and read the labels on medications and clinical equipment. Simulation Instructors may be required to stand or sit and facilitate a simulation scenario for up to 5 hours at a time. In certain simulation events, physicians will be required to perform procedures and participate in clinical maneuvers on mannequins and task trainers. Simulation Instructors will be required to work a minimum of 25 hours in simulation per academic year, with a maximum of 200 hours per academic year. Minimum Qualifications MD or DO. Board Certified. Licensed to practice medicine in Arizona at the time of appointment. Currently in good standing with the medical board of their specialty. Dedicated to the delivery of patient-centered medicine. Available to work a minimum of 25 hours in Simulation per Academic Year. Individuals who are selected for employment and do NOT currently hold a faculty title with The University of Arizona College of Medicine-Phoenix, will be required to obtain faculty status. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Preferred Qualifications Practicing clinician in a medical or surgical specialty that encompasses the content being taught in the Simulation Faculty Track being considered. Previous experience with medical student or graduate medical education preferred. Previous experience in teaching simulation. Demonstrates ability to work as a member of the healthcare team, exhibiting skills, communication practices and behaviors of a team leader. One year of technical experience working in a remote or virtual environment using video conferencing software, preferably Zoom. Rank Tenure Information FLSA Non-Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week Varies Job FTE .025 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $100 Per Hour Compensation Type hourly rate Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 0 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Talent Acquisition, Office of Human Resources talent@arizona.edu Open Date 12/2/2025 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. Cover Letter: Should clearly indicate how your skills and professional employment experience meet the Minimum and the Preferred qualifications (if applicable). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/24453?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req24453 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 11/12/2025 Back to Search Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (T/TE) Posting Number req24453 Department Immunobiology Department Website Link https://immunobiology.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location University of Arizona Health Sciences Address 1656 E. Mabel Street, Tucson, AZ 85724 USA Position Highlights The Department of Immunobiology invites applicants to apply for the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. We are seeking investigators whose research program aligns with a department working to understand how the immune system and/or microbes influence the continuum of healthy and diseased states across the lifespan. We will consider individuals studying areas of microbial biology (viruses, bacteria, parasites, fungi), immunology (normal & aberrant function of the immune system, microbial & cancer immune defense, and related topics), and those whose programs span these disciplines. Applicants combining the above with innovative techniques/methodologies (e.g., molecular engineering), relevant cancer biology, and/or those with relevant translational elements to their program are encouraged to apply. We are looking for collegial & collaborative-minded colleagues. The candidate will be expected to participate in medical and/or graduate teaching & service at the departmental, college, and university levels. Teaching loads vary but are not expected to exceed 30 contact hours per year for research-intensive faculty. Senior & mid-career applicants will be expected to be leaders in their areas, with innovative programs & robust extramural funding. Junior applicants will be expected to establish new, cutting-edge programs & with the help of mentoring programs, secure extramural funding. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, vision, and life insurance; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please Duties & Responsibilities Establish or continue a successful, extramurally funded research program that will advance knowledge in the broad areas of immunobiology, as defined above. Develop and sustain productive interactions with biomedical investigators in the department, the College of Medicine-Tucson, and the University of Arizona. Promote and strengthen interdisciplinary training by contributing to graduate (M.Sc./Ph.D.) and/or medical (M.D.) education. Provide institutional service in the Department of Immunobiology. Additional duties may be assigned. Minimum Qualifications PhD and/or MD or equivalent degree. Peer-reviewed publications in nationally recognized journals\n     and expertise in any field related to immunology, microbiology, parasitology,\n     infectious diseases, and cancer biology. For Associate level or above, active funding in the above. Preferred Qualifications Rank Tenure Information TBD FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 2 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dr. Kristian Doyle | doylekr@arizona.edu or Dr. Deepta Bhattacharya | deeptab@arizona.edu Open Date 11/19/2025 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please provide the following: \n1. Cover letter, 2. CV, 3. NIH-formatted Biosketch, 4. Research Statement and 5-Year plan (limit 3 pages), and 5. Teaching Philosophy (limit 1 page). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/24112?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, Department of Psychiatry (MD and/or PhD) (Phoenix)",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, Department of Psychiatry (MD and/or PhD) (Phoenix) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, Department of Psychiatry (MD and/or PhD) (Phoenix) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req24112 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 11/12/2025 Back to Search Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, Department of Psychiatry (MD and/or PhD) (Phoenix) Posting Number req24112 Department COM Phx Psychiatry Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Medical Sub-Speciality Psychiatry Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 N. 5th St., Phoenix, AZ 85004 USA Position Highlights The Department of Psychiatry at the University of Arizona College of Medicine - Phoenix seeks to recruit faculty specializing in research on the etiology and/or treatment of psychiatric disorders. Reporting directly to the Chair, the preferred candidate will have an established research program specializing in fields such as genomics, transcriptomics, neuroimaging, animal models, pharmacology, or related fields as applied to\nillnesses such as schizophrenia, mood and anxiety disorders, and substance dependence. Applicants must possess a PhD and/or MD (or equivalent) and postdoctoral training in the proposed field of study. Candidates must have prior experience supervising research projects, obtaining and maintaining research budgets, procurement of research equipment and supplies as well as devising and successfully implementing research policies of the organization. A strong track record of publishing peer-reviewed manuscripts and research grant applications is required. The candidate will be required to submit intramural and extramural research grant proposals in the area of their proposed research program. Finally, the candidate will be expected to participate in the educational, training, and service missions of the Department and College. The successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member in the Department of Psychiatry, actively participate in teaching, clinical service and/or scholarly work, and must qualify for an appointment at the rank of assistant, associate, or full professor. The successful candidate will meet the requirements of either a career track or tenure track faculty according to the College and University guidelines. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Biosciences Core (PBC) campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists and leaders who are life-long learners and inquisitive scholars. The PBC campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. Outstanding UA benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Develop and maintain a federally funded and internationally competitive research program in causes and treatments of psychiatric disorders. Publish research findings in leading journals. Develop collaborations with pertinent faculty within the college and partner institutions. Contribute to the teaching and service missions of the college. Engage in external academic and professional activities. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Must possess a PhD and/or MD or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. Must have post-doctoral training in respective area of research and experience commensurate with appointment at the rank of assistant professor or above. Be eligible for a University of Arizona academic appointment at the assistant professor level or higher. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated scholarly accomplishment in research. Demonstrated experience as principal investigator and evidence of active extramural funding. Outstanding record of research accomplishments. Demonstrated experience in submitting grant proposals and supervising research staff. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively. Teaching experience with medical and/or graduate students. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information To be Determined FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay AAMC Public Schools for Rank and Specialty Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 3 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Talent Acquisition, Office of Human Resources talent@arizona.edu Open Date 11/12/2025 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. CV and cover letter is required for to be considered for the position. One additional document will be professional reference contacts. Please include at least three professional references at your current rank or above, their contact information and the nature of your work with them. Faculty selected for this teaching opportunity will be required to obtain faculty status within the appropriate academic department. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27444?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice (MMD), Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Practice (MMD), Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Practice (MMD), Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27444 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/23/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Practice (MMD), Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE) Posting Number req27444 Department Disability Psychoeduc Studies Department Website Link https://coe.arizona.edu/dps Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona's College of Education invites applications for a full-time, career-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Practice in the Mild to Moderate Disabilities (MMD) program within the Department of Disability and Psychoeducational Studies. We are seeking candidates with expertise in special education, particularly in the preparation of teachers who serve students with mild to moderate disabilities in K–12 educational settings. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching, student advising, field supervision, program development, and professional service. Our faculty place a high value on collaboration, collegiality, cooperation, mutual respect, and shared responsibility. We seek an individual who brings enthusiasm, leadership, and a commitment to excellence in teacher preparation and evidence-based educational practices. The MMD program prepares undergraduate and graduate students to become effective special educators serving students with high-incidence disabilities, including learning disabilities, emotional and behavioral disabilities, intellectual disabilities, autism spectrum disorders, and attention-deficit disorders. The program emphasizes evidence-based instructional strategies, assessment, individualized education program (IEP) development, positive behavioral supports, inclusive educational practices, and collaboration with families and other educational professionals. The University of Arizona offers a Bachelor of Science in Education in MMD and a Master of Arts in Special Education with a specialization in MMD. These programs combine academic coursework with practical field experiences, including practica and supervised student teaching, to prepare candidates for professional responsibilities in K–12 special education settings. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction, primarily through online courses, although in-person teaching assignments may also be required. Responsibilities include teaching, advising, and mentoring students; supervising field experiences and student teaching; supporting recruitment and program development; and participating in department, college, and university service. This career-track appointment is not eligible for tenure. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona is situated in beautiful Tucson, Arizona. The Tucson community is surrounded by mountains, hiking trails, and natural recreation areas and offers a warm Southwestern atmosphere. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teach four courses per academic semester in undergraduate and graduate special education programs, primarily through online instruction. In-person teaching assignments may be required based on program needs. Develop, deliver, and evaluate high-quality instruction using evidence-based teaching practices appropriate for preparing K–12 special education teachers. Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students in the Mild to Moderate Disabilities program, including providing academic and professional guidance. Supervise and support students completing field experiences, practica, and student teaching placements in K–12 educational settings. Collaborate with school-based cooperating teachers, university supervisors, and school district personnel to support clinical experiences and teacher preparation. Participate actively in student recruitment, admissions activities, and student retention initiatives. Contribute to curriculum development, course improvement, program assessment, and other activities supporting the continuous development and improvement of the Mild to Moderate Disabilities program. Assist with program-level activities related to teacher preparation, certification, assessment, and compliance with applicable university and state requirements. Actively participate in departmental, college, and university service activities, including committee assignments and other responsibilities. Collaborate with faculty members and program personnel to support program operations, student success, and the overall mission of the Department of Disability and Psychoeducational Studies. Engage in professional outreach and collaboration with schools, educational agencies, and relevant professional organizations at local, state, and national levels. Perform other appropriate duties and responsibilities as assigned. The following list represents possible teaching assignments, depending on the successful candidate's expertise, background, and program needs. Courses may include, but are not limited to: Undergraduate Courses SERP 400: Survey of Exceptional Students SERP 403: Special Services in the Schools SERP 405: Introduction to Learning Disabilities SERP 407A: Special Education Assessment (Elementary) SERP 407B: Special Education Methods: Reading, Writing, and Mathematics (Elementary) SERP 408A: Assessment for Secondary SERP 408B: Methods for Teaching Students with High-Incidence Disabilities at Secondary SERP 475: Practicum SERP 493: Internship (Student Teaching) Graduate Courses SERP 503: Special Services in the Schools SERP 507A: Academic Assessment: Mild to Moderate Disabilities SERP 508A: Mathematics Assessment and Instruction in Special Education SERP 508B: Assessment and Instruction for Struggling Readers SERP 508C: The Science of Reading in Special Education SERP 511A: Basic Academic Skills for Mild to Moderate Disabilities SERP 593: Internship (Student Teaching) Teaching assignments may also include other undergraduate and graduate courses within the Mild to Moderate Disabilities and/or Special Education programs. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Knowledge of evidence-based instructional practices for students with mild to moderate disabilities in K–12 educational settings. Ability to develop and teach high-quality undergraduate and graduate courses in online and traditional instructional formats. Knowledge of special education assessment, IEP development, curriculum adaptation, positive behavioral supports, and inclusive educational practices. Ability to advise and mentor students and supervise preservice teachers in clinical and field-based experiences. Strong interpersonal, organizational, communication, and collaborative skills. Ability to work effectively with students, faculty, school personnel, and other educational stakeholders. Ability to contribute to program development, student recruitment, and university service activities. Minimum Qualifications An earned Ph.D. in Special Education or a closely related field. Possession of an Arizona teaching certificate in Mild to Moderate Disabilities or eligibility to obtain the appropriate Arizona certification. A minimum of three years of K–12 teaching experience in special education settings. Experience with instructional practices, assessment, and educational programming for students with mild to moderate disabilities. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching undergraduate or graduate courses in special education at a college or university, using diverse delivery methods. Experience developing and delivering online or asynchronous university courses. Experience supervising preservice teachers, practica, internships, or student teaching placements. Experience with evidence-based academic and behavioral interventions for students with mild to moderate disabilities. Experience with assessment, individualized instruction, IEP development, and progress monitoring. Experience in inclusive educational settings, co-teaching, and collaboration with general education teachers. Experience in curriculum development, program assessment, or teacher preparation program improvement. Experience with Arizona teacher certification requirements and professional standards for special education teacher preparation. Experience establishing or maintaining partnerships with K–12 schools, school districts, and educational agencies. Experience advising and mentoring undergraduate or graduate students. Experience contributing to recruitment, retention, and student success initiatives. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dr. Sunggye Hong Professor, Disability and Psychoeducational Studies sghong@arizona.edu Open Date 10/23/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant This is a full-time career-track faculty appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor of Practice within the Department of Disability and Psychoeducational Studies. This career-track appointment is not eligible for tenure, but is eligible for promotions in accordance with university policies. Please submit the following three documents: Cover Letter: A letter outlining the applicant's qualifications, including K–12 special education teaching experience, expertise in Mild to Moderate Disabilities, university-level teaching experience, and preparation for the position's teaching, advising, and service responsibilities. Current Curriculum Vitae: A current and complete curriculum vitae. Professional References: Names and contact information, including phone numbers and email addresses, for three professional references. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27407?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research and School of Anthropology",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research and School of Anthropology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research and School of Anthropology Tucson, AZ, United States | req27407 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/23/2026 Back to Search Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research and School of Anthropology Posting Number req27407 Department Tree Ring Laboratory Department Website Link https://ltrr.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research (LTRR) and the School of Anthropology (SoA) at the University of Arizona invite applications for the Emil Haury Endowed Chair in Archaeological Dendrochronology. This is an open-rank search for a tenure-track/tenured faculty position, starting in August 2027. We seek a dendrochronologist to advance and support the field of archaeological dendrochronology via a split appointment in LTRR and SoA. We are looking for a scholar strongly committed to interdisciplinary engagement who will strengthen existing research emphases and/or advance new topics of research, building on key strengths in both departments. A strong commitment to teaching and mentoring students, evidenced by career trajectory and/or track record, is essential. Previous field and/or specialist laboratory experience would be an advantage. Applicants working with ancestral communities in the Americas are particularly encouraged, yet we also welcome strong applications from scholars engaged in any area of archaeological dendrochronology. Applications should be submitted through talent by December 31, 2026. The Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research is a hub of interdisciplinary research, teaching, and outreach at the University of Arizona. Established in 1937, LTRR formalized the scientific study of tree rings in America, pioneering the application of dendroarchaeology in the American Southwest. Using the information preserved in the annual rings of trees, LTRR researchers work to better understand and quantify the complex interactions among Earth's climate, ecosystems, and societies.  Since 2024, the LTRR has expanded its analytical capacity in wood anatomy, stable isotope, and radiocarbon analysis. The laboratory is dedicated to developing and advancing novel approaches to address scientific questions of societal relevance while sharing these discoveries and their impacts with researchers, students, the public, resource managers, and policymakers. The School of Anthropology is a vibrant and collaborative working and learning environment. It is one of the few institutions to fully support the integrated sub-discipline approach to anthropology, with research embracing Archaeology, Biological Anthropology, Linguistic Anthropology, and Sociocultural Anthropology (including Applied Anthropology). Through the School, the University of Arizona provides meaningful opportunities to learn about what is human, how we evolved, and what lies in our future. Emil W. Haury was a pioneering archaeologist and chronologist of the American Southwest. He served as Director of the SoA from 1937 to 1964, fostering strong ties with the LTRR, contributing directly to the development of the Geochronology Laboratories, the Arid Lands Program, and the University of Arizona Press. He built a program of national and international renown. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Establish and maintain an innovative and robust research program, including grant acquisition and scholarly publication, advancing archaeological dendrochronology at national and international levels. Teach 3 three-credit courses per year (2 in SoA and 1 in LTRR), including a combination of undergraduate and graduate classes. Serve on review panels and thesis and dissertation committees for graduate students. Mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Contribute to faculty governance and service at the departmental, college, university, and professional levels. Participate in outreach, student advising, recruitment, and other activities that enhance student engagement, support a welcoming academic community, and expand collaborations with community partners. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. or equivalent in Archaeology, Anthropology, Geography, or a closely related field by December 31, 2026. Demonstrated ability or trajectory to develop research projects and gain funding for Dendroarchaeological research. Publication history commensurate with career stage. Knowledge of current theory, research methodology, and practice in related fields. Demonstrated ability or trajectory to maintain a research program with an active field component and/or specialist laboratory-based analyses. Ability and commitment to develop and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in dendrochronology and anthropology. Preferred Qualifications Prior teaching and mentoring experience at the university level. Interest in contributing to the university’s General Education curriculum and/or to online platforms for instructional delivery. A track record of, or trajectory of public engagement, partnership-building, or outreach with a broad range of communities and/or institutions. Commitment to interdisciplinary engagement. Expertise and research plans to build on existing strengths in LTRR and/or SoA. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates If any clarification or further information is required, please contact: Charlotte Pearson Associate Professor of Anthropology Associate Professor of Dendrochronology clpearson@arizona.edu Open Date 10/23/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following documents: Curriculum Vitae (CV). A 2-5 page cover letter that describes your research, teaching, and outreach experience, as well as your future scholarly plans. The cover letter should include a short statement about the use of generative AI, if any, in preparing the application. Names and contact information for three professional references. Reference letters will be requested at a later date. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27388?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor, Computer Science (T) and Director of Cognitive Sciences",
      "description": "Associate or Full Professor, Computer Science (T) and Director of Cognitive Sciences | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate or Full Professor, Computer Science (T) and Director of Cognitive Sciences Tucson, AZ, United States | req27388 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/23/2026 Back to Search Associate or Full Professor, Computer Science (T) and Director of Cognitive Sciences Posting Number req27388 Department Computer Science Department Website Link https://cs.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Computer Science at the University of Arizona invites applications for a tenured faculty position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. The successful candidate will conduct research on artificial intelligence, have interdisciplinary interests, and serve as Director of the Cognitive Science Program. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research spans one or more of the following areas: artificial intelligence, multimodal AI, computer vision, visualization and visual perception, machine learning, natural language processing, speech recognition, human-centered computing, human-computer interaction, and computational cognitive modeling. Applications of these areas to health, broadly construed, would be of particular interest. The Department of Computer Science has a long history of research accomplishments, influential software distribution, and substantial external funding. The department currently has 18 tenure-track faculty and 13 teaching faculty. We offer three undergraduate degrees (B.S. and B.A. in Computer Science and B.S. in Artificial Intelligence) and two graduate degrees (M.S. and Ph.D. in Computer Science). Current research areas include algorithms, bioinformatics, computational geometry, data management, human-computer interaction, machine learning, natural language processing, networks, programming languages, security, computer vision, and visualization. The Computer Science department is in the College of Science, which includes other highly ranked departments such as Astronomy and Geosciences, with which Computer Science faculty actively collaborate. The Cognitive Science program serves as an interdisciplinary hub, bringing together many affiliated faculty members from various departments including Computer Science, Information Sciences, Psychology/Neuroscience, Linguistics, Speech Language & Hearing Sciences, Education, and Philosophy. Among them are 8 and growing core faculty members, jointly appointed between the CogSci program and their home department. The CogSci program offers a joint undergraduate major (B.S. in Neuroscience and CogSci) and a graduate minor in CogSci. Students and affiliated faculty in the community actively collaborate and regularly come together for interdisciplinary activities. Review of applications will begin on the preferred application deadline of November 16, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The University of Arizona is located in the heart of Tucson, the second largest city in Arizona. The Tucson metro area has over one million people, has its own international airport, and is close to Phoenix with a population of over five million. Tucson is known for its stunning Sonoran Desert landscape. It is surrounded by saguaros and 4 mountain ranges and has ample opportunities for leisure activities, including amazing biking, hiking, rock climbing, horseback riding, and caving. Tucson is one of 49 UNESCO Cities of Gastronomy, and has a vibrant music and art scene. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities The faculty member will develop and maintain a top-quality research\nprogram, teach graduate and undergraduate courses, and perform service to the\ndepartment, field, and wider community. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the position start\ndate. Preferred Qualifications FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Fingerprint criminal background check (security sensitive due to job duties) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/17/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Faculty Recruiting Committee Chair search@cs.arizona.edu Open Date 10/23/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following documents: Cover Letter. Curriculum Vitae. Research Statement (upload in the “Additional Document” slot). Teaching Statement (upload in the \"Additional Document\" slot), should include evidence of teaching effectiveness. Reference Contact Information (upload in the \"Additional Document\" slot), listing contact information for a minimum of three professional references. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27422?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Career Track Faculty, BSN-Integrative Health (Gilbert) (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions)",
      "description": "Career Track Faculty, BSN-Integrative Health (Gilbert) (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Career Track Faculty, BSN-Integrative Health (Gilbert) (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions) Gilbert, AZ, United States | req27422 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Career Track Faculty, BSN-Integrative Health (Gilbert) (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions) Posting Number req27422 Department College of Nursing Department Website Link https://nursing.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 92 W Vaughn Ave , #301, Gilbert, AZ 85233 USA Position Highlights Join our nationally ranked, CCNE-accredited pre-licensure nursing program as a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Principal Lecturer OR Assistant/Associate/Full Clinical Professor (FT) and help shape the future of nursing education. We're seeking passionate nurses for our integrative health BSN program who are committed to innovative teaching and interdisciplinary collaboration. Make a meaningful impact in nursing education—apply today and become part of a supportive, dynamic academic environment driving excellence in healthcare. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teaching: Teach and guide students in theoretical and clinical practice courses within assigned program. Mentor students to support their academic success and professional growth. Create and maintain a positive, nursing values–driven learning environment. Evaluate student performance and contribute to ongoing curriculum development. Engage in program planning and assessment through faculty governance and continuous quality improvement. Collaborate with faculty, staff, and clinical partners to strengthen academic-practice relationships. Ensure compliance with program, college, and clinical site policies. Scholarship: Maintain clinical proficiency through direct or indirect practice. Develop academic excellence through practice-relevant inquiry and knowledge dissemination via publications and presentations. Contribute to grant efforts supporting student education and training. Service: Engage with faculty, the administrative leadership team, and students in furthering the mission and goals of the College of Nursing. Participate in special projects and faculty governance activities, as needed. There may be additional duties assigned as they relate to this position. Minimum Qualifications Rank to be determined by the college’s Faculty Appointment Promotion & Tenure (FAPT) Committee. All ranks: Graduate degree in nursing or a related field (must have a BSN, if graduate degree is non-nursing). Two (2) years of clinical experience as RN providing direct patient care. Evidence of teaching and clinical practice or systems leadership experience, through certification, or equivalent preparation. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree (PhD, EdD, or DNP) in nursing or a related field (required for professorial track). Experience teaching maternal-child, OB, pediatrics, or fundamentals in entry-to-practice nursing programs. Recent direct or indirect nursing practice. Experience in nursing simulation and student instruction. Experience in curriculum design, program evaluation, and active learning methods. Proficient in curriculum design, program evaluation, and active learning methods. Certified by a nationally recognized credentialing agency. Experience with online teaching methodologies. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 28-40 Job FTE 0.7-1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 2 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Lauren Acosta | heller@arizona.edu Open Date 10/21/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit all requested documents and complete the application in its entirety. In your Cover Letter, please address your teaching interest and/or experience with maternal-child, OB, pediatrics, or fundamentals. \nCandidates are encouraged to review the College of Nursing appointment criteria for the Career-Track Lecturer Series and Career-Track Professorial Series. Applicants who wish to be considered for appointment at a specific or advanced rank should ensure that their CV and Cover Letter clearly demonstrate evidence consistent with the applicable rank criteria. Appointment series and rank will be determined based on the College of Nursing criteria for appointment to the Career-Track Lecturer or Career-Track Professorial series. Documents needed to apply: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Unofficial Academic Transcripts Proof of Current RN Licensure Names and Phone Numbers of at Least Three (3) Professional References Other: Provide evidence/receipt of an active Arizona RN License prior to first day of employment with College of Nursing. Maintain own license, immunizations, CPR, and other agency or College requirements and provide evidence of current status. Must be eligible to obtain a Level 1 Fingerprint Clearance Card from AZDPS. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27415?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Practice (Theriogenology & Clinical Skills) (Oro Valley, AZ)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Practice (Theriogenology & Clinical Skills) (Oro Valley, AZ) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate/Professor of Practice (Theriogenology & Clinical Skills) (Oro Valley, AZ) Oro Valley, AZ, United States | req27415 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate/Professor of Practice (Theriogenology & Clinical Skills) (Oro Valley, AZ) Posting Number req27415 Department Vet Med Administration Department Website Link https://vetmed.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Outside of Tucson Address 1580 E. Hanley, Oro Valley, AZ 85737 USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona College of Veterinary Medicine (U of A-CVM), located in Oro Valley, Arizona, invites applications for a faculty position focused on teaching veterinary theriogenology within the Doctor of Medicine (DVM) program. We are seeking a veterinarian with clinical and instructional experience in large animal veterinary theriogenology to design coursework and lead discussion primarily in the theriogenology/endocrinology course. Additional similar contributions would be anticipated in relevant topics across the pre-clinical curriculum. In addition, this faculty member will guide the development and delivery of large animal portions of the longitudinal Clinical Skills curriculum for first and second year veterinary students through active learning and case-based instruction. This career-track appointment is not eligible for tenure and selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States. The University of Arizona College of Veterinary Medicine is shaping the future of veterinary professionals by preparing graduates to tackle today’s challenges confidently. By emphasizing competency-based, hands-on, student-centered learning, the college has become a national leader in veterinary medical education. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members;  retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Design, develop, and deliver veterinary theriogenology content for the systems-based Cycle of Life (theriogenology/endocrinology) course with emphasis on large animal reproduction. Similar smaller contributions anticipated within other courses (both foundational and elective) where appropriate across the DVM curriculum. Collaborate closely with faculty and instructional technicians to develop, refine, and deliver engaging, hands-on learning experiences in theriogenology. Partner with Clinical Skills team members (faculty and staff) in the design, development, delivery, and refinement of large animal experiences in the Clinical Skills courses within the pre-clinical veterinary medical doctorate curriculum which are focused on general day-one ready skillsets not related to theriogenology. Demonstrate clinical skills and assess student learning through commitment to objective structured clinical exams (OSCEs): develop rubrics for and launch assessments of hands-on skills as opportunities to “check in” with students and provide constructive feedback on opportunity areas for growth. Collaborate with faculty leading organ-system block\n     courses to ensure integration of the clinical skills curriculum across the\n     continuum of the program and deliver curriculum content to veterinary\n     students within the CVM vision of inclusive, evidence-based Team-Based\n     Learning and Peer Instruction pedagogy. Provide regular out-of-class support through office\n     hours, mentoring, and scheduled meetings to assist a diverse student body\n     in achieving learning outcomes. Mentor and support students as a faculty advisor for student\n     clubs, fostering professional growth and engagement. Participate\nactively in the CVM and university communities through engagement in shared\ngovernance, advisory committees, curriculum improvement, and relevant academic\nor professional societies. Minimum Qualifications DVM/VMD degree (or international equivalent) from an AVMA accredited college of school. At\nleast two years of clinical experience in veterinary theriogenology. Preferred Qualifications Expertise in large animal theriogenology. At least five years of clinical practice experience. Experience mentoring students, with a preference on mentoring veterinary students. Prior experience in an academic veterinary teaching institution. Board certification or board eligibility in a relevant specialty such as American College of Theriogenologists. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Frankie Kolb I fkolb@arizona.edu Open Date 10/21/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27394?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Communication (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Communication (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Communication (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27394 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/20/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Communication (T/TE) Posting Number req27394 Department Communication Department Website Link https://comm.arizona.edu/ Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Communication at the University of Arizona, invites applications for a tenure-eligible Assistant Professor position starting in Fall 2027. Successful candidates will take a social scientific approach to their research using quantitative and/or qualitative methods. We seek candidates who will: (a) contribute to the department’s research mission in communication, with an active program of research in an area that complements existing department strengths in health, technology, interpersonal, organizational/group, intercultural/intergroup, political, social influence, or mass communication; (b) contribute to the department's undergraduate teaching mission in public relations and strategic communication by offering courses such as PR Ethics and Law, PR Research Methods, PR Writing, PR Campaigns, Crisis Communication, or Nonprofit PR, as well as offering additional PR courses in their area of expertise; (c) contribute to the department's graduate program in communication by advising graduate students in their area of specialty at the MA and PhD levels and by offering graduate-level communication courses. The Department is committed to empirical communication research, either basic or applied, making original and substantively important contributions, and is regularly ranked among the top communication research programs in the country. More information about the communication department is at www.comm.arizona.edu . Application review will begin December 1, 2026. This position is anticipated to start on August 16, 2027. The final candidate determination will take into consideration the candidate’s availability to start in alignment with the beginning of the semester. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Contribute to the department’s existing research mission in communication, and teaching mission in public relations and strategic communication. Minimum Qualifications Applicants should have a Ph.D. in Communication or a closely related discipline. ABD candidates will be considered, but the doctorate must be conferred by the start date. Evidence of a strong research program in communication. Evidence of teaching excellence in PR/strategic communication and/or communication more broadly. Experience with or potential to engage in our graduate program. Preferred Qualifications A record or potential for obtaining external research funding. Accreditation in Public Relations (APR) credential. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Josephine Almazan Administrative Associate, Department of Communication josephinealmazan@arizona.edu Open Date 10/20/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Two Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please upload the following documents with your application: CV. Cover letter describing your fit for the position and outlining your scholarly research identity and future trajectory. A statement of teaching philosophy, which must include evidence of teaching effectiveness, information related to teaching undergraduate PR classes, and your approach to supporting student learning, access, and success among students from a broad range of backgrounds and experiences (in \"additional document\" slot). The names and contact information for three references who will be contacted for a letter of recommendation if needed (in \"additional document\" slot). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27390?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Director, MD/PhD Program (Tenure Track) (MD/PhD), Department of Basic Medical Sciences (COM-P)",
      "description": "Director, MD/PhD Program (Tenure Track) (MD/PhD), Department of Basic Medical Sciences (COM-P) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Director, MD/PhD Program (Tenure Track) (MD/PhD), Department of Basic Medical Sciences (COM-P) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req27390 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Director, MD/PhD Program (Tenure Track) (MD/PhD), Department of Basic Medical Sciences (COM-P) Posting Number req27390 Department COM Phx Basic Medical Sci Department Website Link https://phoenixmed.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 N 5th Street , Phoenix, AZ 85004  USA Position Highlights The College of Medicine – Phoenix (College) is seeking applications for a director of the MD/PhD Program. Applicants should be experienced clinician scientists with a strong track record of research, mentoring, and program leadership to direct, develop, and grow the current MD/PhD program. The medical school was established in 2007 in Phoenix as an independent four-year allopathic program. The MD/PhD track was launched in 2017 and has steadily grown to support students in a culture that promotes a collaborative and dynamic platform for medical education, biomedical research and clinical innovation. This growth parallels the College's expanding research portfolio. The program supports trainees pursuing rigorous dual-degree training across the translational spectrum. The program benefits from a highly collaborative tri-institutional environment, including the University of New Mexico and the University of Arizona programs (Colleges of Medicine in Phoenix and Tucson) as part of the Southwest Clinical and Translational Science Award (SW CACTI). This infrastructure allows students access to additional resources in clinical research design, regulatory science, biomedical informatics, community engagement, and mentorship networks. The College has extramural research funding during the previous five years to over $50 million, including $23 million in NIH funding. The director will provide leadership, oversight, and coordination of the overall program, including recruitment, admissions, orientation, course design, program evaluation, and financial management. The successful applicant will be a physician-scientist with a strong track record of excellence in teaching and research, including leadership in educational programs. They should have the ability to manage student portfolios and demonstrate longitudinal engagement and guidance between the MD and PhD program components. Outstanding communication skills and collaboration with other academic, research, and medical/biomedical programs across the campus will be integral to the program’s continued success. The director’s role will be up to 0.50 FTE. It is expected that the faculty will spend the other 0.50 FTE focused on other mission-based activities as determined by the Department of Basic Medical Sciences. The successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member in the Department of Basic Medical Sciences, actively participate in research, teaching and/or clinical service, and must qualify for an appointment at the rank of associate or full professor. The successful candidate will meet the requirements of tenure-track faculty according to the College and University guidelines. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Biosciences Core (PCB) campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists, and leaders who are lifelong learners and inquisitive scholars. The PBC campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; state retirement plan; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Provide administrative oversight,\n       growth strategies and assessment for the MD/PhD Program. Responsible for oversight of the\n       MD/PhD students. Provide mentorship for all MD/PhD\n       students. Organize, develop and participate\n       in MD/PhD Program meetings to establish policies and procedures,\n       assessment and evaluation systems, academic progress tracking and\n       integration across key components of the MD program. Promote the program by\n       participating in public presentations and conducting outreach to\n       potential students. Work effectively with the Office of\n       Admissions and Recruitment to review applications, interview applicants,\n       arrange second look visits and prepare recommendations for acceptance. Lead efforts to update\n       communication and websites related to the program. Work closely with the College of\n       Medicine – Phoenix leadership to ensure smooth transitions between the\n       MD and PhD segments of the program and timely completion of graduation\n       requirements. Apply for Federal funding to\n       support the MD/PhD Program. Knowledge,\n  Skills, Abilities: Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to convey complex information clearly and effectively. Demonstrated knowledge of and commitment to health sciences and clinical education. Demonstrated experience working effectively with a range of communities, building professional relationships, and adapting communication and engagement approaches to meet varying needs. Minimum Qualifications Hold an MD and PhD from an\n     accredited institution. Have or be qualified for a\n     faculty appointment at the College in the rank of associate or full\n     professor. Be eligible for unrestricted\n     medical licensure in the State of Arizona. Be eligible for clinical\n     privileges at one of the following clinical partners: Banner – University\n     Medical Center Phoenix, Phoenix Children’s or Phoenix VA Health Care\n     System. Have a track record of\n     publishing research findings. Preferred Qualifications Be a graduate of a dual MD/PhD program. Demonstrate experience leading a combined degree program. Have five (5) or more years of research experience as a faculty member. Show experience in mentoring trainees. Demonstrate experience in developing meaningful assessment and evaluation programming. Have experience with administrative leadership. Have management experience. Demonstrate experience in graduate student education, with recognized strength in and experience with mentoring graduate students and/or postdoctoral fellows in research. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay AAMC Public Schools for Rank and Specialty Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Office of Talent Acquisition talent@arizona.edu Open Date 10/19/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Two Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missing information may be considered an incomplete submission. CV, cover letter, and two additional documents are required to be considered for the position. One additional document will be a teaching philosophy statement, which should clearly indicate your interest in this role and what skills or professional employment experience you have to support your application. The other additional document will be professional reference contacts. Include at least three professional references at your current rank or above, their contact information and the nature of your work with them. Faculty selected for this teaching opportunity will be required to obtain faculty status within the appropriate academic department. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27387?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Research Professor, Immunology (Non-tenure Eligible) (MD, PhD, MD/PhD)",
      "description": "Research Professor, Immunology (Non-tenure Eligible) (MD, PhD, MD/PhD) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Professor, Immunology (Non-tenure Eligible) (MD, PhD, MD/PhD) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req27387 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Research Professor, Immunology (Non-tenure Eligible) (MD, PhD, MD/PhD) Posting Number req27387 Department COM Phx Basic Medical Sci Department Website Link https://phoenixmed.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 N 5th Street, Phx., Phoenix, AZ 85004 USA Position Highlights The Department of Basic Medical Sciences (BMS) at the University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix seeks to hire assistant, associate, or full professors in the broad area of immunology. The positions report directly to the department chair. The Department of Basic Medical Sciences at the College of Medicine – Phoenix is undergoing a significant and strategic expansion in the research enterprise. The department is seeking to recruit a full research professor to complement and enhance the current cadre of highly innovative, collegial, collaborative, and well-funded group of faculty within the department and college. Currently, the department's strengths include neuroscience, cancer, metabolic, and cardiovascular diseases. This position includes overseeing and conducting an independent research program, conducting collaborative and programmatic research with other faculty, publishing findings in peer-reviewed journals, pursuing and securing extramural funding, and taking on duties and responsibilities that demonstrate strong service to the department and the college. This may include serving on committees, task forces, coordinating, overseeing, or assisting in specific aspects of departmental operations. The successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member within the Department of Basic Medical Sciences, actively participate in teaching, service, and scholarly work, and qualify for appointment as full professor. Candidates with strong research programs in immunity to infection, inflammation, and systems immunology are strongly encouraged to apply. Candidates with research programs not centrally related to immunology and inflammation will not be considered. The successful candidate will meet the requirements of a non-tenure-eligible track faculty member according to the College and University guidelines. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Biosciences Core (PBC) campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists, and leaders who are lifelong learners and inquisitive scholars. The PBC campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This career-track appointment is not eligible for tenure. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Develop innovative and collaborative immunology-related\n     research. Obtain extramural, including federal, support for research\n     programs and publish high impact scientific research in the broad areas of\n     immunology-related research. Develop collaborations with pertinent faculty within\n     the College and partner institutions; for senior candidates, lead larger\n     programmatic initiatives (P, U and similar grants) in their related\n     fields. Contribute to the teaching (medical, graduate and/or\n     other education), mentoring and service components of the College’s\n     missions. Other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Ability to mentor and guide students and trainees in clinical research projects. Strong interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, with the ability to interact professionally and effectively with colleagues, students, trainees, and research partners. Minimum Qualifications Hold an MD, PhD or MD/PhD or an\n     equivalent degree from an accredited institution in biological sciences,\n     medicine or a related field. Exhibit a track record of high impact publications, extramural research funding and teaching. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrate innovation and federally funded research in immunology and/or systems immunology. Have a strong track record in teaching and mentoring in classroom or laboratory settings. Have experience in an academic medical center and medical education environment. Exhibit a strong record of peer-reviewed publications in high-impact journals. Demonstrate experience with new techniques and approaches not currently available at the College of Medicine – Phoenix. Have experience serving on the editorial board of a peer-reviewed scientific journal. Rank Professor Tenure Information Non-Career-Track (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 8 Job FTE .20 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible To be Determined Rate of Pay AAMC Public Schools for Rank and Specialty Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates HR-PHX@arizona.edu Open Date 10/19/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missing information may be considered an incomplete submission. A CV, cover letter, and two additional documents are required to be considered for the position. One additional document will be a statement of research interest, which should clearly indicate your interest in this role and what skills or professional employment experience you have to support your application. The other additional document will be professional reference contacts. Please include at least three professional references at your current rank or above, their contact information, and the nature of your work with them. Faculty selected for this teaching opportunity will be required to obtain faculty status within the appropriate academic department. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27381?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Limited Term Adjunct Instructor (Multiple Positions)",
      "description": "Limited Term Adjunct Instructor (Multiple Positions) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Limited Term Adjunct Instructor (Multiple Positions) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27381 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Limited Term Adjunct Instructor (Multiple Positions) Posting Number req27381 Department Disability Psychoeduc Studies Department Website Link https://education.arizona.edu/disability-psychoeducational-studies Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Disability and Psychoeducational Studies (DPS) at the University of Arizona is seeking qualified educators to join our faculty as non-tenure-track Limited Term Adjunct Instructors for part-time teaching opportunities during the academic year. Note: Teaching assignments and schedules will depend on departmental needs and course availability. Assignments may be online, in person, or hybrid. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teach undergraduate or graduate courses within the Department of Disability and Psychoeducational Studies, as assigned based on departmental needs and the instructor’s qualifications. Plan and deliver instruction, including lectures, activities, assignments, and assessments. Develop and maintain course materials in accordance with program and university expectations. Evaluate student work and provide timely, constructive feedback. Support students through field experiences, practica, internships, or other experiential learning activities, as applicable. Collaborate with DPS faculty, staff, community partners, and other stakeholders. Apply effective and engaging instructional practices to support varied student learning needs. Participate in required course, program, or departmental meetings and activities. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Ability to use effective and engaging instructional strategies in higher education that support varied student learning needs. Ability to support students with varied backgrounds and experiences in their academic and professional development. Ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, community partners, and other stakeholders. Strong interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated commitment to reflective practice, continuous professional development, and student success. Ability to work independently and collaboratively and to share instructional strategies and materials. Ability to acquire and apply the knowledge and skills necessary to serve as an effective instructor. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree or equivalent graduate-level preparation in disability studies, psychoeducational studies, education, educational psychology, special education, counseling, school psychology, Deaf Studies, educational interpreting, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Demonstrated professional experience in one or more areas related to Counseling, Special Education, School Psychology, Educational Interpreting, Deaf Studies, or another relevant discipline. Demonstrated experience in teaching, training, supervision, or instruction in higher education or a comparable professional setting. Demonstrated experience developing and delivering engaging instruction for students with varied backgrounds, experiences, and learning needs. Demonstrated experience using learning-management systems and instructional technology, as required by the assigned course. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree or significant progress toward a doctorate in a relevant field. College or university teaching experience, preferably in an area of disabilities and psychoeducational studies. Experience teaching online, in-person, and/or hybrid courses. Experience developing course materials, assessments, and other instructional resources. Professional licensure, certification, or specialized credentials relevant to the assigned course or area of instruction. Experience supervising students in practicum, internship, field placement, clinical, or other experiential-learning settings. Demonstrated experience working with varied learners and communities, including students with disabilities. Evidence of professional development, scholarly activity, or current professional practice in a relevant discipline. For assignments in specialized areas, such as Deaf Studies or educational interpreting, demonstrated proficiency and current experience in the applicable content area, including ASL skills or interpreting credentials when appropriate. Rank Instructor Tenure Information Adjunct (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 8 Job FTE .20 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $5,120 Compensation Type Per Course Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 3 Target Hire Date Expected End Date 5/14/2027 Contact Information for Candidates Jina Yoon Department Head, Disability and Psychoeducational Studies jinayoon@arizona.edu Open Date 10/19/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Please upload the following documents with your application: Current Curriculum Vitae. Cover Letter. In the additional document slot, enter the names and contact information for THREE references, including phone numbers and email addresses. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27346?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (T/TE), Educational Psychology and Cognitive Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (T/TE), Educational Psychology and Cognitive Science | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor (T/TE), Educational Psychology and Cognitive Science Tucson, AZ, United States | req27346 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor (T/TE), Educational Psychology and Cognitive Science Posting Number req27346 Department Educational Psychology Department Website Link https://education.arizona.edu/educational-psychology Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Educational Psychology in the College of Education at the University of Arizona, in partnership with Cognitive Science, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning in August 2027. We seek a scholar who studies human cognition in AI- and data-rich environments, with applications to learning and education. We are particularly interested in scholars whose work examines cognitive processes such as decision-making, reasoning, memory and metacognition, or cognitive biases and applies this understanding to questions such as how people interpret data and AI-generated information, evaluate risk and probability, make data-informed decisions, or interact with AI-supported systems. This position is part of an interdisciplinary cluster titled “Understanding the Human Intelligence Behind the Use of Artificial Intelligence.” The broader initiative connects Educational Psychology, Cognitive Science, Information Science, Public Health, and the Humanities around shared questions about using AI to recommend reform, best practices, and data-based decisions in P-16+ formal and informal education settings. The cluster emphasizes making AI and data-intensive systems interpretable, trusted, behaviorally informed, and effective in real-world human systems with a focus on mental health, wellness, and learning. We therefore especially welcome scholars whose work bridges basic and applied research and who are interested in interdisciplinary collaboration. Formal review of applicants will begin November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here. Duties & Responsibilities The position is a joint appointment between the Department of Educational Psychology in the College of Education and Cognitive Science in the College of Science. The successful candidate will be positioned to connect these communities, bringing cognitive perspectives to interdisciplinary work on AI, data, learning, and educational systems. The Department of Educational Psychology (https://education.arizona.edu/educational-psychology) conducts research and graduate training in learning and cognition, human development, motivation, measurement, and research methodology. Faculty use a range of experimental, quantitative, qualitative, and mixed methods approaches to study learning and behavior in educational and other real-world settings. The department is also expanding its work in areas such as artificial intelligence and data literacy. The Cognitive Science Program (https://cogsci.arizona.edu/) serves as an interdisciplinary hub, bringing together many affiliated faculty members from various departments including Computer Science, Information Sciences, Psychology/Neuroscience, Linguistics, Speech Language & Hearing Sciences, Education, and Philosophy. A small but growing group of core faculty members are jointly appointed between the CogSci program and their home department. The CogSci program offers a joint undergraduate major (B.S. in Neuroscience and CogSci) and a graduate minor in CogSci. Students and affiliated faculty in the community actively collaborate and regularly come together for interdisciplinary activities. Located in the beautiful desert Southwest, Tucson is nestled between the Catalina and Tucson Mountains and Saguaro National Park. This bicycle-friendly city is known for its arts, music, food, and rich history. Tucson is a designated UNESCO City of Gastronomy and is consistently ranked as one of the country’s best places to live. Duties & Responsibilities Establish and maintain an active, independent research program that advances understanding of human cognition and decision making, particularly in AI- and data-rich environments, with applications to learning and education. Pursue external research funding through grant proposals. Develop collaborations within and across disciplines, particularly as they related to the cluster hire initiative. Disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, and other scholarly activities. Teach graduate and undergraduate courses, including in-person and online courses in Educational Psychology and Cognitive Science, with opportunities to contribute to instruction in data literacy. The expected teaching load is 3 classes per academic year. Advise and mentor master's and Ph.D. students by supporting their research and professional development. Actively participate in departmental, college, university, and interdisciplinary program service. Minimum Qualifications PhD in Educational Psychology, Cognitive Science, Cognitive Psychology, Learning Sciences, or a closely related field by the start date. Outstanding record of research, as evidenced by a notable publication history and a demonstrated record of seeking and securing funding. Commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring in Educational Psychology (with some emphasis in data literacy) and Cognitive Science at both the undergraduate and graduate level. Ability to contribute to interdisciplinary research and collaboration across Educational Psychology and Cognitive Science. Preferred Qualifications Experience conducting research in educational or other real-world settings. Experience teaching and mentoring students from a range of cultural and linguistic backgrounds and/or conducting multilingual or cross-cultural research. Rank Assistant Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Jonathan Tullis, PhD Associate Professor, Educational Psychology tullis@arizona.edu Open Date 10/19/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Four Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Applications will be reviewed starting November 15, 2026 and will be accepted until the position is filled. Please submit the following application materials: Curriculum vitae Cover Letter specifically addressing the applicant's match to the criteria described above. Statement of Research describing current scholarly activities and future plans Statement of Teaching Philosophy and Experience Three publications demonstrating a focus on one or more of the research areas listed above (combined into one PDF) Three references, including names and contact information. If advanced, we will request letters of recommendation from your references. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27343?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Biochemistry (T/TE) (Multiple Positions)",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Biochemistry (T/TE) (Multiple Positions) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant or Associate Professor, Biochemistry (T/TE) (Multiple Positions) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27343 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Assistant or Associate Professor, Biochemistry (T/TE) (Multiple Positions) Posting Number req27343 Department Chemistry & Biochemistry - Sci Department Website Link https://cbc.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry (CBC), within both the College of Medicine – Tucson and the College of Science at the University of Arizona, invites applicants for an Assistant or Associate Professor tenure track position. The successful candidate will join a dynamic research environment within CBC, with opportunities to collaborate with numerous departments and centers of excellence across the University. We seek candidates with a research and education program that is focused on biochemistry or structural biology. Preference will be given to candidates with interests and expertise in experimental and/or computational biochemistry or structural biology of nucleic acids or related systems, with an emphasis on research problems related to cancer. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience working with a variety of student populations, stakeholders, and colleagues. Candidates must be committed to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and to fostering a welcoming environment that supports students, trainees, staff, and colleagues from a broad range of backgrounds. Candidates will be expected to lead a vigorous and sustainable research program, as demonstrated by an excellent track record of creative research accomplishments and external funding. Priority will be given to candidates with demonstrated potential to collaborate and participate in activities across campus that meet the strategic goals of the Department, Colleges, and University. Individuals who apply should be committed to collaboration and to working with faculty members in advancing research and practice within the program and across disciplines. We seek individuals who bring enthusiasm, leadership capability, and a strong commitment to academic excellence within the scholar-practitioner tradition. Priority consideration will be given to applications received by November 15, 2026. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Establish, maintain, and lead a vigorous, productive, nationally and internationally recognized research program in biochemistry or structural biology. Teach at the graduate and undergraduate levels and contribute to innovation in curricular development. Mentor students in an effective and respectful manner. Participate in outreach and contribute to department, colleges, and university service. Develop innovative approaches to enhancing student engagement and expanding collaborations with community and business partners. Knowledge,\nSkills, and Abilities (KSAs): Demonstrated excellence in research and education related to biochemistry or structural biology. Demonstrated leadership of research and outreach activities. Ability to attract and maintain external research funding. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Minimum Qualifications PhD in Biochemistry, or a closely related field. Postdoctoral research experience. Relevant publications in the field. Evidence of successful acquisition of research funding. Demonstrated experience mentoring students. Preferred Qualifications Translational research experience. Demonstrated leadership in large-scale research endeavors. Experience engaging with industry partners. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 2 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Lisa Arrotta Executive Assistant lisaarrotta@arizona.edu Open Date 10/19/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Two Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following documents: Curriculum Vitae (CV). Cover Letter. Statement of research plans (up to 6 pages, including a 1-page executive summary). Teaching and mentoring statement highlighting experiences and goals related to mentoring individuals from a broad range of backgrounds (up to 2 pages). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27339?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Department Head, Speech, Language & Hearing Sciences (Professor, T/TE)",
      "description": "Department Head, Speech, Language & Hearing Sciences (Professor, T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Department Head, Speech, Language & Hearing Sciences (Professor, T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27339 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/16/2026 Back to Search Department Head, Speech, Language & Hearing Sciences (Professor, T/TE) Posting Number req27339 Department Speech Language & Hearing Sci Department Website Link https://slhs.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona’s Department of Speech, Language, & Hearing Sciences invites applications for a tenured or tenure-eligible faculty position at the rank of Full Professor in communication sciences and disorders. The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s research, teaching, and service missions while also serving as Department Head. This is a nine-month academic-year position beginning in August 2027, with salary commensurate with experience. Additional compensation associated with the Department Head role, including summer salary, will be arranged with the Dean of the College of Science. Responsibilities include departmental administration as well as research, teaching, and service. The Department seeks a faculty member with research expertise in any area of Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences who is committed to leading the department in a manner that supports faculty, staff, students, and the broader local community. The Department of Speech, Language, & Hearing Sciences is renowned for its highly engaged undergraduate students, top-ranked clinical training programs in Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology, an active research doctoral program, and cutting-edge research. The department includes 30 tenure-track and career-track faculty members, 104 graduate students across its M.S., Au.D., and Ph.D. programs, and 189 undergraduate students. Faculty in the department benefit from rich opportunities for collaboration across the University of Arizona and the Tucson community. Housed within the College of Science and connected with the School of Mind, Brain, and Behavior, the department benefits from a broad network of scientists across multiple disciplines. Ongoing collaborations include faculty in Neuroscience, Psychology, Cognitive Science, Education, Biomedical Engineering, Music, and other fields. The department has collaborative relationships with local healthcare leaders, including Banner Medical Center, Tucson Medical Center, and the Southern Arizona Veterans Affairs Health Care System. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona is a research intensive (R1) land-grant institution that has received a federal designation as a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI). Arizona is home to 22 federally recognized tribes, with Tucson being home to the O’odham and the Yaqui. The university strives to build sustainable relationships with sovereign Native Nations and Indigenous communities through education offerings, partnerships, and community service. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities The\nproposed workload for this position: 50% Administrative 20% Scholarly Activity/Research 20% Service 10% Teaching Administrative\nduties for this position include: Serving as the liaison between the department, the college, and the university’s central administration Representing the department at departmental, college, and university events Leading and directing the department, with input from the departmental community, in the following areas: Departmental and teaching budgets Funding opportunities Space and equipment allocation Teaching assignments Formation and appointment of departmental committees National accreditation and academic program review processes Curriculum development and student advising Promotion and tenure processes Recommendations to the Dean regarding salary increases Hiring, termination, and evaluation of faculty and staff Communication with faculty in accordance with departmental bylaws Coordination and collaboration with Associate Department Heads, the Advisory Committee, and Clinical Program Directors/Coordinators Research\nand scholarship activities include but are not limited to: Establishing and maintaining a successful research program in the field of Speech, Language, and/or Hearing Sciences Maintaining a consistent record of peer-reviewed publications and presentations Engaging in community-based research partnerships Pursuing extramural funding Connecting departmental faculty with potential research collaborators within and outside the university Mentoring students in research, from undergraduate students through postdoctoral fellows Teaching\nactivities may include: Teaching classroom-based courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels Providing effective, evidence-based instruction that fosters critical thinking and motivates independent learning Service\nactivities include, but are not limited to: Participating effectively on departmental, college, and university committees Holding meaningful appointments or serving in local, state, national, or international scientific, professional, government, or community organizations related to communication disorders and the research or service needs of individuals with communication disorders Minimum Qualifications PhD\n     in Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences or a related field Demonstrated expertise in an area relevant to Communication Sciences and Disorders, as evidenced by a record of scholarly work, including peer-reviewed publications and presentations, as well as a record of high-impact research and extramural funding commensurate with appointment at the rank of Full Professor Preferred Qualifications Preferred Qualifications and Leadership Characteristics include: Demonstrated leadership experience and a commitment to effective leadership practices Clinical certification (CCC) in Speech-Language Pathology or Audiology preferred Demonstrated experience mentoring faculty, commensurate with the level of appointment Ability and commitment to collaborate effectively with multidisciplinary teams of researchers and clinicians across career levels Experience teaching at the college or university level FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Fingerprint criminal background check (security sensitive due to title or department) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Julie E. Miller, PhD Associate Professor Depts. of Neuroscience and Speech, Language & Hearing Sciences Chair, Faculty Search Committee juliemiller@arizona.edu Open Date 10/16/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Four Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following documents: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Contact information for three professional references. (References will only be contacted if you are a finalist.) Department Head Statement describing prior leadership experience and vision (2-page maximum) Research Statement (2-page maximum) Teaching Statement describing classroom experience and vision for supporting departmental teaching overall (2-page maximum) Additional materials may be requested from applicants selected for the on-campus interview. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
