    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27230?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Department of Neurosurgery | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Department of Neurosurgery Tucson, AZ, United States | req27230 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Department of Neurosurgery Posting Number req27230 Department Neurosurgery Department Website Link https://medicine.arizona.edu/neurosurgery Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address 1501 N. Campbell Ave., Tucson, AZ 85719 USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona's Department of Neurosurgery seeks a dynamic and collaborative research faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Tucson delivers cutting-edge programs in medical education, highly innovative and collaborative research opportunities, as well as advanced patient care in an environment where inclusive excellence creates a foundation for community-responsive action. Founded on the campus of the University of Arizona in 1967 as the state’s first MD degree-granting college and a resource for the people of Arizona, today the UArizona College of Medicine ranks among the top medical schools in the nation for research, teaching, and primary care. With 22 Departments and 9 Centers, the College of Medicine students, faculty, staff, and alumni today continue more than 50 years of service in advancing medical care, biomedical research, and knowledge in Arizona — and around the world. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; state retirement plan; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Care\n     for patients with neurosurgical disease in the operating room, clinic and\n     wards. Aid in\n     development of an academic neurovascular program. Train\n     and supervise residents and students in clinical settings. Organize\n     and lead multi-disciplinary research efforts. Supervise\n     and mentor graduate, medical, and undergraduate students in research\n     endeavors. Collaborate\n     on teaching and research endeavors related to diseases of the brain with\n     other departments. Submit\n     grants with the goal of funding agreed upon portion of salary after start\n     up period. Contribute\n     to the MD/PhD program. Add\n     meaningfully to the peer reviewed literature. Participate\n     in Neurosurgical organizations on a regional and national level. Liaise with marketing and Philanthropy to\nincrease visibility and funding. Follow\n     College of Medicine – Tucson organizational protocols, policies, and\n     procedures. Ensure\n     compliance with all pertinent laws and regulations including the Health\n     Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) and Human Subjects\n     Protection Program (HSPP). Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications MD/PhD Demonstrated track record of distinction in\nresearch in an academic research department. Preferred Qualifications Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dr Julie Pilitsis jpilitsis@arizona.edu Open Date 10/8/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missing information may be considered an incomplete submission. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/25884?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Chief, Division of Arthroplasty, Department of Orthopaedics (MD) (College of Medicine - Phoenix)",
      "description": "Chief, Division of Arthroplasty, Department of Orthopaedics (MD) (College of Medicine - Phoenix) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Chief, Division of Arthroplasty, Department of Orthopaedics (MD) (College of Medicine - Phoenix) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req25884 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 5/1/2026 Back to Search Chief, Division of Arthroplasty, Department of Orthopaedics (MD) (College of Medicine - Phoenix) Posting Number req25884 Department COM Phx Orthopedics Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Medical Sub-Speciality Arthroplasty Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 North 5th Street, Phoenix, AZ 85004 USA Position Highlights The chief of the Division of Arthroplasty in the Department of Orthopaedics will provide strategic leadership and direction for all aspects of the Division. The chief of Arthroplasty will be responsible for all planning, policy, operations, and oversight of the Division's academic, research, and clinical programs. This leader will have financial authority over the state-appropriated budget assigned to the Division, extramural research funding received, philanthropic funding, and other Division-related financial matters, in accordance with applicable University policies and practices. The chief will have discretion in the recruitment and retention of Division faculty, who will report directly to the chief of the Division of Arthroplasty. This position reports directly to the chair of the Department of Orthopaedics within the College of Medicine – Phoenix for all academic activities. Consistent with the Academic Affiliation Agreement (AAA) between the University of Arizona and Banner Health, the chief will have a matrix reporting relationship to the CEO of Banner – University Medical Group (B—UMG) and CEO of Banner – University Medical Center Phoenix (B—UMCP) for all clinical activities conducted at B—UMCP. This role provides strategic, clinical, and academic leadership for the Division while maintaining a robust clinical practice focused on adult reconstruction and joint replacement. The chief will foster excellence in patient care, education, research, faculty development, and ambulatory growth aligned with the missions of the University of Arizona and Banner Health. The successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member in an appropriate department, actively participate in teaching, clinical service, and/or scholarly work, and must qualify for an appointment at the rank of associate or full professor. The successful candidate will meet the requirements for a career-track faculty member as outlined by the College and University. This career- track appointment is not eligible for tenure. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Biosciences Core (PCB) campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists, and leaders who are lifelong learners and inquisitive scholars. The PBC campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; state retirement plan; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Provide strategic   and operational leadership for the Division of Arthroplasty, ensuring   alignment with departmental, college and Banner Health priorities. Lead   divisional efforts in quality, safety, patient outcomes and clinical   standardization, including joint replacement pathways and value-based care   initiatives. Partner   with departmental and BUMG leadership on ambulatory growth strategies,   access, clinic efficiency and care model optimization. Support faculty   recruitment, onboarding, mentoring and professional development within   the Division. Oversee   divisional performance metrics, including volume, quality outcomes, patient   experience and financial stewardship. Collaborate   with hospital and health system leaders on service line development,   technology adoption and program expansion. Participate   in departmental and institutional leadership committees as appropriate. Maintain   an active adult reconstruction/arthroplasty clinical practice, including   primary and revision hip and knee arthroplasty. Provide   high-quality patient care in ambulatory clinic settings, surgical   services and inpatient care at Banner – University Medical Center Phoenix. Actively participate in multidisciplinary   care teams, focusing on enhanced recovery after surgery (ERAS), perioperative   optimization and value-based care. Serve as a   referral resource for complex arthroplasty cases within the Banner system. Teach and   mentor medical students, residents and fellows in orthopedic   surgery and related programs. Contribute   to curriculum development and educational innovation within the Department of   Orthopaedics. Support   scholarly activity, including clinical research, outcomes research or   educational scholarship, consistent with academic rank and interests. Promote a   culture of inquiry, professionalism and continuous improvement. Minimum Qualifications Hold an MD or an equivalent\n     degree from an accredited institution. Be eligible for a University of\n     Arizona academic appointment at the associate professor level or higher. Be board certified in\n     speciality. Be eligible for unrestricted\n     medical licensure in the State of Arizona. Be eligible for medical staff\n     membership at Banner – University Medical Center Phoenix. Have a successful track record\n     of publishing research findings in arthroplasty. Preferred Qualifications Exhibit greater than 10 years of progressively complex administrative experience (e.g., program leader, center/section director, etc.) in an academic medical center setting. Have leadership experience supervising orthopedic faculty and staff. Demonstrate outstanding interpersonal communication and professionalism skills. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week varies Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay AAMC Public Schools for Rank and Specialty Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Office of Talent Acquisition talent@arizona.edu Open Date 5/1/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Resume, Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. CV, cover letter, and one additional document required to be considered for the position. One additional document will be professional reference contacts. Please include at least three professional references at your current rank or above, their contact information, and the nature of your work with them. Faculty selected for this teaching opportunity will be required to obtain faculty status within the appropriate academic department. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information, including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and the policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27265?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Limited Term Adjunct Lecturer- Marriage & Family Therapy (Multiple Positions)",
      "description": "Limited Term Adjunct Lecturer- Marriage & Family Therapy (Multiple Positions) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Limited Term Adjunct Lecturer- Marriage & Family Therapy (Multiple Positions) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27265 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/27/2026 Back to Search Limited Term Adjunct Lecturer- Marriage & Family Therapy (Multiple Positions) Posting Number req27265 Department School of Human Ecology-RES Department Website Link Norton School of Human Ecology Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The John and Doris Norton School of Human Ecology seeks to hire up to four Limited Term Adjunct Lecturer positions to teach graduate courses in the Marriage & Family Therapy (MFT) program. These positions involve some course development and teaching within the main campus MFT program at The University of Arizona. All course modalities will be in-person and the dates of the course will be in either Summer or Fall 2027. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teach courses in the state-accredited Marriage & Family Therapy curriculum including but not limited to some of the following courses: HECL 560: Play Therapy (offered in summer 2027 13-week term from 5/17/2027 --8/11/2027) HECL 570: Interventions with Parents & Children (offered in summer 2027 13-week term from 5/17/2027 --8/11/2027) HECL 600: Analysis & Treatment of Human Sexual Development (offered in Fall 2027 from 8/23/2027 --12/8/2027) HECL 620: Advanced Family Therapy (offered in Fall 2027 from 8/23/2027 --12/8/2027) Deliver the course in-person on the main campus. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in Marriage & Family Therapy or other related discipline. 5 years or more clinical experience in the mental health field, including diagnosis, assessment, and treatment of mental health challenges. Teaching and mentoring experience in academic contexts and/or clinical settings. Preferred Qualifications Experience in instructional design and delivery. Experience working with Arizona Board of Behavioral Health Examiners. Familiarity with COAMFTE accreditation standards. Rank Lecturer Tenure Information Adjunct (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 8 Job FTE .20 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $7,000 Compensation Type Per Course Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 4 Target Hire Date 5/17/2027 Expected End Date 12/19/2027 Contact Information for Candidates Darin Knapp darink@arizona.edu Open Date 10/27/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Resume and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27499?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Pharmacology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Pharmacology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Pharmacology Tucson, AZ, United States | req27499 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/29/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Pharmacology Posting Number req27499 Department Pharmacology Department Website Link https://pharmacology.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Pharmacology Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Pharmacology invites applications for a tenure-eligible position at the rank of Assistant Professor. We seek a translational sensory neurobiologist investigating how peripheral sensory neurons initiate and sustain pain and itch, with a research program that works directly in human sensory neurons alongside preclinical models. This recruitment is intended to extend the department's established strength in pain and analgesic pharmacology by adding capacity for human tissue–based target identification and validation. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the Comprehensive Center for Addiction and Pain, as well as to the training of graduate students in the department’s PhD program in Medical Pharmacology. Visa sponsorship is available for this positions. Outstanding UA benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Seek extramural funding for research by developing and submitting research grant proposals to appropriate funding agencies; as Principal Investigator, partner with faculty to form research teams, develop research plans, write funding proposals, and implement and dissemination of results through peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, and invited lectures. Develop independent research programs and contribute to graduate (PhD) and medical (MD) education. Engage in teaching activities for undergraduate and graduate students. Participate in appropriate departmental and/or College of Medicine committees, and professional organization committees as appropriate. Additional duties may be assigned. Minimum Qualifications PhD, MD, or MD/PhD in pharmacology, neuroscience, physiology, or a closely related discipline. Postdoctoral training in somatosensory neurobiology with a record of first-author publications in peer-reviewed journals. Demonstrated expertise in patch clamp electrophysiology of primary afferent neurons, including recordings from human dorsal root ganglion neurons. Demonstrated expertise in multiphoton calcium imaging of sensory neurons. An active individual peer-reviewed NIH career development award as principal investigator, held at the time of appointment. Demonstrated research program addressing sex differences in pain mechanisms, evidenced by first-author peer-reviewed publications. Preferred Qualifications For Early Career Assistant Professor Rank, current peer reviewed funding (K99, K22 or equivalent). Experience integrating pharmacological screening with multiphoton calcium imaging in an ex vivo skin–nerve preparation for comprehensive phenotyping of functionally identified sensory neuron populations. Research program spanning both pain and itch modalities.\nExpertise in neurohormonal signaling as a driver of nociceptor sensitization and pain amplification. Experience with voltage-gated sodium channel physiology and pharmacology across multiple pain states, including traumatic nerve injury and chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy. Funding aligned with the NIH HEAL Initiative or comparable federal priorities in non-opioid analgesic discovery. Experience with rodent models of post-surgical pain. Rank Tenure Information Tenure-Eligible (TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dr. Todd Vanderah, vanderah@arizona.edu Open Date 10/29/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Two Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. Cover Letter: Please outline your qualifications, research focus, and appropriateness for the position. Curriculum Vitae: Please provide details of your accomplishments including publication record, current and planned funding, teaching experience, and service. Statement of Research Interests: Please summarize your prior research interests and future directions. Statement of Teaching Interests: Please describe your teaching experience and teaching philosophy. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27492?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Practice, Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27492 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Practice, Disability Psychoeducational Studies (Career-Track) (COE) Posting Number req27492 Department Disability Psychoeduc Studies Department Website Link https://coe.arizona.edu/dps Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Disability and Psychoeducational Studies in the College of Education at the University of Arizona invites applications for an Assistant Professor of Practice position in Rehabilitation Counseling. This career-track (non-tenure eligible) position combines teaching, student mentoring, and rehabilitation workforce development through an intergovernmental service agreement (ISA) with the Department of Economic Security-Arizona Rehabilitation Services Administration (DES AZRSA). The successful candidate will collaborate closely with AZRSA and community partners to identify workforce learning needs and develop relevant continuing education and professional development opportunities for rehabilitation personnel. The successful candidate will also teach, advise, and support students and non-matriculated AZRSA personnel in the Counseling degree program-Rehabilitation Counseling emphasis. The Clinical Rehabilitation Counseling and Clinical Mental Health Counseling specializations are ranked No. 1 in Arizona, and the Clinical Rehabilitation Counseling specialization ranks among the top ten nationally. The Ph.D. program emphasizes research and scholarship among faculty, students, and graduates, consistent with our R1 institutional designation. The B.S.E. is an applied degree that prepares advocates and human service specialists with a comprehensive knowledge of the rehabilitation process. The M.A. and Ph.D. degrees are accredited by CACREP through 2032. The University of Arizona is situated in beautiful Tucson, Arizona. The Tucson community is surrounded by mountains, hiking trails, natural recreation areas, and exudes a warm Southwestern atmosphere. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Serve as Principal Investigator on the intergovernmental\nservice agreement (ISA) with the Department of Economic Security Arizona\nRehabilitation Services Administration (DES-AZRSA) and oversee its\nimplementation and administration. Build and maintain collaborative\nrelationships with DES-AZRSA leadership and personnel, university colleagues,\nand community partners to advance rehabilitation education, workforce\ndevelopment, and the goals of the ISA. Resubmit five -year cycle ISA\nproposals and assist with budget development and management, allocation of\ntuition support and stipends, recruitment and advising of AZRSA students, and\ncompletion of required student and ISA documentation. Teach five courses\nannually, which may include ISA-related courses and other courses within the\ncounseling programs. Teaching assignments and their distribution across terms\nwill be determined in consultation with program and department leadership. Advise and mentor AZRSA students and non-students, and oversee academic\nprogress. Develop and update curricula to reflect student learning needs,\nrehabilitation practice, workforce priorities, and changes/trends in AZRSA as\nwell as the profession. Collaborate on applied research and grant proposals\nthat inform rehabilitation practice and workforce development. Participate in\nfaculty meetings and serve on department, college, and university committees. Minimum Qualifications Earned doctorate in counseling or a closely related field by\nthe start date. Professional experience in vocational rehabilitation,\nrehabilitation counseling, or closely related disability and employment\nservices. Strong communication, organizational, and collaborative problem-solving\nskills, including the ability to coordinate activities across university and\nagency settings. Possess or be eligible to obtain the Certified\nRehabilitation Counselor (CRC) credential. Preferred Qualifications Experience or demonstrated potential in assessing workforce learning needs and developing, delivering, or evaluating continuing education, professional development, or training for adult learners. Experience or demonstrated potential in teaching and mentoring undergraduate students, graduate students, or working professionals, including through online or hybrid formats. Experience with program development, program evaluation, program coordination, project management, budgets, grants, contracts, or related administrative responsibilities. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Michael Hartley mthartley@arizona.edu Open Date 10/28/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant The position will begin on August 16, 2027. This career-track appointment is not eligible for tenure, but is eligible for promotions. Please submit the following three documents: Cover Letter- Outlining your qualifications for this position Current Curriculum Vitae – Current and complete Name and Contact Information of THREE References (phone and email) Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27440?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/ Full Professor, Computer Science (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/ Full Professor, Computer Science (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate/ Full Professor, Computer Science (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27440 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate/ Full Professor, Computer Science (T/TE) Posting Number req27440 Department Computer Science Department Website Link https://cs.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Computer Science at the University of Arizona invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in all areas and at all ranks. The Department of Computer Science has a long history of research accomplishments, influential software distribution, and substantial external funding. The department currently has 18 tenure-track faculty and 13 teaching faculty. We offer three undergraduate degrees (B.S. and B.A in Computer Science and B.S. in Artificial Intelligence) as well as two graduate degrees (M.S. and Ph.D. in Computer Science). Current research areas include algorithms, bioinformatics, computational geometry, data management, human-computer interaction, machine learning, natural language processing, networks, programming languages, security, computer vision, and visualization. The Computer Science department is in the College of Science, which includes other highly ranked departments such as Astronomy and Geosciences, with which Computer Science faculty actively collaborate. Review of applications will begin on the preferred application deadline of November 15, 2026 and continue until the positions are filled. Outstanding UA benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities The faculty member will develop and maintain a top-quality\n  research program, teach graduate and undergraduate courses, and perform\n  service to the department, field, and wider community. Conduct and maintain an active research program in Computer Science. Contribute to the department’s research accomplishments and pursue opportunities for external research funding. Teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Collaborate with faculty within the Department of Computer Science on research initiatives. Participate in interdisciplinary research collaborations with faculty across the College of Science. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Computer Science or closely related field by the\n  position start date. Preferred Qualifications Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure-Eligible (TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Christian Collberg Faculty Recruiting Committee Chair search@cs.arizona.edu Open Date 10/28/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please upload: 1.\tCover Letter 2.\tCurriculum Vitae 3.\tResearch Statement (in “Additional Document” slot) 4.\tTeaching Statement (in \"Additional Document\" slot) 5.\tReference Contact Information (in \"Additional Document\" slot), that lists contact information for a minimum of three professional references. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27303?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (TE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor (TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27303 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor (TE) Posting Number req27303 Department Pharmacology and Toxicology Department Website Link https://www.pharmacy.arizona.edu/departments-directory/pharmacology-toxicology-department Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The R. Ken Coit College of Pharmacy at the University of Arizona in Tucson invites applicants for a tenure-track position at the level of Assistant Professor in the Department of Pharmacology and Toxicology. Qualified candidates will have research interests broadly focused within the fields of toxicology or environmental health. We seek applicants with the capacity to establish a widely recognized, externally funded independent research program, while also contributing to the College’s teaching and mentoring mission in toxicology and environmental health. The department has a strong history of leadership in toxicology and environmental health, with close ties to the NIEHS and the Society of Toxicology. Applicants using artificial intelligence, machine learning, or other computational approaches to solve problems in toxicology or environmental health are encouraged, including work aligned with the University of Arizona’s AI-Driven Health Innovation Initiative. Applicants with traditional laboratory-based research programs are also welcome to apply. The successful candidate will join a department with current research strengths in pharmacology, toxicology, environmental health, medicinal chemistry, and pharmaceutics. We seek a colleague who will contribute to our community by collaborating across those research areas, taking part in undergraduate and graduate training, and helping to strengthen the department’s programs. Excellent research infrastructure and opportunities exist for collaborations with researchers across the University of Arizona, such as the Southwest Environmental Health Sciences Center, the Superfund Research Program, One Health Research Initiative, Training in Environmental Toxicology of Human Disease T32, Coit Center for Longevity and Neurotherapeutics, Southwest Center on Resilience for Climate & Health, Bio5 Institute, and the Arizona Cancer Center. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Provide instruction in professional, graduate, and undergraduate degree programs. Develop interdisciplinary research programs. Maintain a strong extramurally-funded research program. Actively participate in internal and external committees as needed. Produce strong peer-reviewed publications Lead or contribute to “team science” based interdisciplinary research grants/projects. Minimum Qualifications PhD, PharmD, or MD degree. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated track record of presentations and peer-reviewed journal publications in toxicology, environmental health, or related field. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure-Eligible (TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Nathan Cherrington | cherring@arizona.edu Open Date 10/28/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Please include a letter of interest (including the names of three references), and a 2-page research overview along with your CV & Cover Letter. Please submit your application materials by December 1, 2026. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27226?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Neuroscience, Gut-Brain Axis (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Neuroscience, Gut-Brain Axis (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Neuroscience, Gut-Brain Axis (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27226 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Neuroscience, Gut-Brain Axis (T/TE) Posting Number req27226 Department Neuroscience Department Website Link https://neurosci.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Departments of Neuroscience and Molecular and Cellular Biology at the University of Arizona invite applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. This joint appointment is part of the University’s AI Innovation in Health initiative, which advances interdisciplinary discovery at the intersections of neuroscience, molecular and cellular biology, nutrition, metabolism, and artificial intelligence. We seek an innovative scientist investigating fundamental mechanisms through which the gut, its microbial communities, and the brain interact to shape neural function, behavior, metabolism, health, and disease. The successful candidate will establish a rigorous, independent, and externally funded research program and contribute to a collaborative intellectual community spanning both departments and the broader AI Innovation in Health initiative. The successful candidate’s research program should meaningfully integrate artificial intelligence, machine learning, computational modeling, multi-omics, or other advanced quantitative methods to generate or test biological hypotheses and deepen mechanistic understanding. Relevant research areas include, but are not limited to: The microbiome–gut–brain axis and the mechanisms that support bidirectional communication between the gastrointestinal system and the nervous system; Microbial metabolites and their effects on neural circuits, cellular physiology, behavior, or disease processes; Gut-derived metabolic, immune, or endocrine signaling and its influence on the brain and peripheral nervous system; Neuroimmune interactions and inflammatory mechanisms linking the gut, microbiome, and nervous system; Inter-organ communication among the gut, brain, liver, adipose tissue, immune system, and other physiological systems; Neural and molecular mechanisms governing feeding, appetite, energy balance, and metabolic homeostasis; and Effects of diet, nutrition, and metabolism on brain function, development, aging, and neurological or neuropsychiatric disease. About the University of Arizona The University of Arizona is committed to research, education, and service that address important scientific and societal challenges. As a land-grant institution, the University connects discovery and learning with public engagement and practical benefit. The successful candidate will join a community that values rigorous scholarship, interdisciplinary collaboration, excellent teaching, and mentorship across career stages. Collaborative Environment The University of Arizona offers a highly collaborative environment for research across neuroscience, molecular and cellular biology, physiology, nutrition, data science, and related fields. The joint appointment will create opportunities to build connections across the Department of Neuroscience, the Department of Molecular and Cellular Biology, and other University programs and centers whose strengths align with the candidate’s research, such as the BIO5 Institute. Additionally, the successful candidate will have graduate faculty status in the Biochemistry & Molecular and Cellular Biology PhD Program, as well as the Graduate Interdisciplinary Programs of Neuroscience and Genetics. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute actively to this interdisciplinary community through collaborative scholarship, shared training, and intellectual leadership. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until January 3, 2027, for an anticipated start date of August 16, 2027. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Establish and maintain a rigorous, innovative, nationally recognized, and externally funded independent research program. Conduct research that advances understanding of gut–brain and microbiome biology and complements strengths in Neuroscience, Molecular and Cellular Biology, and the Food as Medicine Initiative. Meaningfully incorporate or develop artificial intelligence, machine learning, computational modeling, multi-omics, or advanced quantitative methods within the research program. Engage in collaborative research and interdisciplinary scholarship across the University of Arizona. Demonstrate excellence and innovation in undergraduate and graduate teaching and contribute to the educational missions of both departments. Provide thoughtful, effective mentorship to undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral trainees and support their development as independent scientists. Participate in departmental, college, and University service. Advance the University of Arizona’s land-grant mission through education, outreach, community engagement, or the translation of scientific discovery in ways that benefit Arizona and its communities. Minimum Qualifications A PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent terminal degree in neuroscience, molecular or cellular biology, microbiology, physiology, computational biology, or a related discipline by the anticipated start date. A record of rigorous and impactful research appropriate to career stage. Evidence of the potential to establish an innovative, independent, and externally funded research program. A demonstrated commitment to high-quality teaching, trainee mentorship, collaboration, and inclusive academic excellence. Preferred Qualifications Research expertise addressing the microbiome–gut–brain axis or related mechanisms of inter-organ communication. Experience integrating experimental biology with artificial intelligence, machine learning, computational modeling, multi-omics, or advanced quantitative analyses. A strong publication record and evidence of scientific creativity and independence. Potential to develop collaborations across neuroscience, molecular and cellular biology, metabolism, nutrition, immunology, microbiology, and data science. Evidence of effective teaching, mentoring, outreach, public engagement, or translation of research. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Haijiang Cai, Ph.D. Professor and Associate Department Head Department of Neuroscience haijiangcai@arizona.edu Open Date 10/28/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Applicants should submit the following materials through the University of Arizona’s online employment portal: A curriculum vitae (CV). A cover letter describing interest in the position and alignment with the joint appointment and Food as Medicine initiative. A statement of current and future research plans, including the role of AI, machine learning, computational modeling, multi-omics, or advanced quantitative methods. A statement describing teaching interests and philosophy, evidence of teaching excellence, trainee mentorship, and potential contributions to the University’s land-grant mission. Contact information for three professional references. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27505?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Instructor, Engineering Capstone Design Program (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions)",
      "description": "Instructor, Engineering Capstone Design Program (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Instructor, Engineering Capstone Design Program (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27505 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/29/2026 Back to Search Instructor, Engineering Capstone Design Program (Part-Time) (Multiple Positions) Posting Number req27505 Department Engineering Academic Affairs Department Website Link engineering.arizona.edu Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Craig M. Berge Engineering Design Program of the College of Engineering is seeking instructors to teach a “Cross-disciplinary Design” (ENGR 498A & B) course. This is a two-semester College-wide capstone design course generally taken by graduating seniors in a variety of majors. Students enrolled in the course work in interdisciplinary teams to solve mostly industry-sponsored real-world design problems using industry-standard design processes. The class culminates in a public showcase event (Craig M. Berge Engineering Design Day) at the end of the academic year (Spring and Fall semesters) where projects are reviewed by judges for grades and awards. Instructors will coordinate sections of student teams, mentor students, instruct in the design process, and evaluate progress of assigned project teams (6 or more project teams of around 5 to 7 students per team) over two consecutive semesters. The primary responsibility is to serve as the instructor of record for these teams and autonomously keep teams and projects on track. The ideal candidate will have experience in an industry setting as a project manager or equivalent with emphasis on managing design projects. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teach\n  one section that includes six teams of ENGR 498 each in a variety of\n  settings: Prepare and deliver\n       instructional materials and guide/lead class activities in 90-minute\n       breakout sessions with at least six teams in the section (twice a week on\n       Tuesday’s and Thursday’s from 3:30 – 5:45 OM in both Spring and Fall\n       semesters) to ensure successful individual and team progress in the\n       course and eventual completion of projects assigned to the teams. Deliver instructional\n       materials related to instructor’s areas of expertise for use in the\n       breakout section. This may include written material, videos, or other\n       instructional materials. Evaluate and grade design\n       reports, design presentations, and engineering notebooks in line with\n       course requirements and individual project scopes. Collaborate with course\n       coordinator and 498 other Instructors to ensure uniform evaluation and\n       synchronization of projects. Provide professional and if\n       possible technical instruction to teams with regard to the quality of\n       their designs and the progress of their projects. Create an open\n       communication channel with the program sponsors and ensure that the\n       teams are providing them with regular status updates. Assist in the professional\n       development of students as they transition from academia into careers. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's\ndegree in an engineering or closely related discipline and 5 years of\nexperience as an engineering professional in one or more industrial\norganizations; OR Master's degree in engineering or a closely related\ndiscipline. Preferred Qualifications Ten or more years of experience as an engineering professional in one or more industrial organizations. Experience with the structure and format of courses at the University of Arizona. Experience in working in multidisciplinary teams in corporate settings. Extensive experience in managing engineering projects in corporate settings. Experience with formal project management methods and metrics. Experience demonstrating good presentation and public speaking skills. Professional Engineer certification. Experience working in industry on engineering-related projects. Demonstrated experience with techniques and topics related to engineering design through coursework or industry experience. Experience with conceptual designs and development of technical approaches. Experience with prototyping and building a variety of engineering products. Experience with Industry Safety standards and practices including evaluating material safety and test and evaluation safety practices. Rank Instructor Tenure Information Adjunct (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 10 Job FTE .25 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $48,951 Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 3 Target Hire Date 1/4/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Larry HeadLarry Head Director, Craig M. Berge Engineering Design Program klhead@arizona.edu Open Date 10/29/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27429?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Religious Studies and Classics (Career-Track)",
      "description": "Lecturer, Religious Studies and Classics (Career-Track) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Lecturer, Religious Studies and Classics (Career-Track) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27429 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/29/2026 Back to Search Lecturer, Religious Studies and Classics (Career-Track) Posting Number req27429 Department Religious Studies and Classics Department Website Link https://religion.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Learning Services Building, 1512 E. First Street, Tucson, AZ 85721 USA Position Highlights The Department of Religious Studies and Classics at the University of Arizona is accepting applications for a Lecturer in Religious Studies. The Department is seeking candidates who have a demonstrated record of teaching excellence; who are prepared to teach in-person and online; and who are skilled in recruiting students to the study of religion. The successful candidate will teach general education courses for undergraduate students across a range of academic disciplines. The specialization is open, but the Department is seeking generalists prepared to teach undergraduate courses that enhance the areas of expertise currently in the Department. Teaching competency areas of interest include, but are not limited to, introduction to world religions; religion and health/medicine; religion and law; religion and violence/nonviolence; religion and gender; religion and entrepreneurship; applied religious studies/humanities. This career-track appointment is not eligible for tenure. The Department of Religious Studies and Classics welcomes applicants committed to meaningfully introducing undergraduates to religious studies, demonstrating its value and intersections with other academic disciplines. Applicants with experience teaching online are especially welcome to apply. Alumni Jacquelynn and Bennett Dorrance have made a gift commitment of $5.4 million to endow the deanship of the University of Arizona College of Humanities to bring the humanities to the forefront of the University of Arizona's life and mission through a continuous and fearless spirit of open inquiry: https://humanities.arizona.edu/about/fearless-inquiries-project . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teach 4 courses per semester based on department needs. Hold office hours. Prepare teaching materials independently and collaboratively. Attend meetings and/or events as requested. Actively recruit majors and minors to Religious Studies (BA) and Religious Studies\n       for Health Professionals (BS). Contribute to departmental efforts to develop innovative approaches to enhancing student engagement and fostering a supportive learning environment. Perform additional duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Skill in communicating in English, both written and oral, at a professional level. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Religious Studies or closely related\nfield by August 1, 2027. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching Religious Studies at the university level, especially general education courses typically offered for undergraduates. Experience with online teaching and/or developing online or hybrid courses in line with University of Arizona online pedagogy standards (based on “Quality Matters”). Rank Lecturer Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Courtney Friesen Professor, Religious Studies / Classics friesen@arizona.edu Open Date 10/29/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Please upload 3 required documents for full consideration (please do not type directly into the application system). Curriculum vitae (CV). Cover letter detailing the applicant’s preparation to teach religious studies general education courses, specific areas of teaching competency, and approach to recruiting undergraduates to the major or minor in Religious Studies & Religious Studies for Health Professionals. Teaching Portfolio, including 1-2 sample syllabi and evidence of teaching excellence (such as course surveys, student comments, peer observations) combined into one attachment. Within a few business days after submitting your application, you will receive an email asking you to provide contact information for 3 references (do NOT click the \"Submit\" button until you have added all 3 references using the plus \"+\" sign button). Your references will then be requested to upload a letter of reference into the system. Once the 3 reference letters have been uploaded, the application will be considered complete. Full consideration will be given to complete applications. Review of applications begins on January 15, 2027. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27465?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Mathematics (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Mathematics (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Mathematics (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27465 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Mathematics (T/TE) Posting Number req27465 Department Mathematics Department Website Link https://www.math.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Mathematics at the University of Arizona invites applications for a tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level in statistics or data science, broadly construed. The School of Mathematical Sciences at the University of Arizona encompasses the Department of Mathematics (where faculty have their primary appointments) as well as the Graduate Interdisciplinary Programs (GIDPs) in Statistics and Data Science and in Applied Mathematics. The department offers doctoral, master’s, and bachelor’s programs in Mathematics with several emphases ranging across core mathematics, applied mathematics, statistics and data science, and math education. The GIDPs offer interdisciplinary PhD programs and support research with faculty members from over 20 departments on campus. The University of Arizona is the flagship campus of Arizona’s university system, it is in the “Highest Research Activity” (R1) category of the Carnegie Classification, and is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU). The University is known for its strong programs across the sciences and for its varied student body. Tucson is notable for its enriching heritage (learn more at: Tucson City of Gastronomy ), its beautiful natural setting, and its strong connections to the university (learn more at: Visit Tucson ). Review of applications will begin November 5, 2026, and will continue until the positions are filled. The expected start date is August 16, 2027. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Faculty members are expected to participate in both undergraduate and graduate education (including teaching core graduate courses in the PhD program in Statistics), advise and mentor students, and to lead students to academic success, including those from a broad range of backgrounds. The normal teaching load for research-active faculty is nine units for the academic year (typically three one-semester courses each year). Faculty members will maintain active research programs, publish their work in highly regarded journals, and seek external funding to support the department’s research and academic missions. Faculty members will also participate in outreach and contribute to departmental, college, and university service. In these and other ways, faculty members will help develop innovative approaches to enhancing student engagement, increasing broad participation, and expanding collaborations with community and business partners. Minimum Qualifications Candidates\nare expected to have a PhD by the time of appointment, an excellent research\nrecord and potential commensurate with their career stage, and a strong\ncommitment to teaching, outreach, and educational excellence. Preferred Qualifications Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates For further information, please contact: Alejandra Gaona Administrative Associate Email: agaona@arizona.edu Phone: 520-621-2868 Open Date 10/28/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant In addition to a cover letter and curriculum vitae (CV), please submit: A publication list. A statement on research accomplishments and goals. A statement on teaching experience and goals, including experience in implementing educational excellence in teaching practices. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    }
